hello in this video we'll talk about the  process of cellular locomotion cellular  locomotion is a result from the  coordination of motions generated by  different parts of the cell  cell locomotion underlies various  physiological processes like neutrophil  extraversation wound healing and the  cell migration even that takes place in  the embryonic brain  so let's understand the process of cell  migration in bit more details  so the property which is exhibited by a  moving cell is the polarity that means  the anterior part of the cell is very  different from the rear part  so in the anterior part of the cell  the the movement is initiated by  formation of a large broad membrane  protrusion known as the lamellae podium  and there are  finger like projections coming out from  the anterior part of the cells and these  are known as phyllopodium  both these structures are really  important in cellular locomotion if we  zoom inside these structures we can find  branched actin structures in the  lamellipodium and the actin bundles  present in the phylopodium  so overall it tells us that actin  microfilament network is really  important for cellular locomotion  now let's look at the cell from the side  view and try to understand what happens  when the cell tries to move  in fast moving cells such as fish  epidermal cells movement begins with the  extension of  one or more lamellipodium  and if we zoom in  in the anterior part of the cell we can  see actin networks are now growing and  kind of pushing the cells  forward  so  there are certain molecules such as  prophylene and cofilin regulates these  actin dynamics in the anterior part  profiling severed the actin filament  and increase the solubilization of actin  whereas prophylene helps in  polymerization of the actin  now both these movements are really  important during cell migration  lamellipodium and phylopodium creates a  stable contact with the underlying  surface and it is preventing the  membrane from retracting  now there are focal adhesions between  the surface and several attachment in  the cells  now let's zoom into the anterior part  and try to look at the leading edge of  the cell in the leading edge of the cell  there would be detachment  of the membrane  and this membrane would move a little  bit ahead and form a new adhesion  likewise the cell  body that means the portion that harbors  the nucleus and cytoplasm would  eventually push itself forward  and now if we focus on the posterior  part of the cell it would now detach  and thereby the cell moves one step  forward  this set of movements are repeated  while a cell is migrating in this  animation you can see how the cells are  migrating by repeated repeating this  particular set of movements  now let's try to understand what are the  factors that govern these kind of  cellular  so there are set of  monomeric g proteins such as rac rho  cdc42 all are related to ras  and these set of proteins are known to  be crucial for cellular movement  mutations associated with these gdps  lead to severe defect in cell migration  there are classical experiments which  helps us to understand how these  movement can re can be orchestrated by  several  components  scientists have found that  adding fgf or fibroblast growth factor  lead to formation of  membrane ruffles which are actually  protrusions upward protrusions of the  membrane  now when rack which is discussed earlier  which is a gtpas is micro injected  into the fibroblast immediately almost  immediately  membrane ruffle forms  it takes some time  for the focal adhesion and the stress  fiber to form  so that tells us there are two sets of  events which are temporarily  differentiated might be regulated by  different factors  later on scientists found that if they  add inactivated rack then the effect of  fgf is abolished  here we put fgf in the media at the same  time inactivated rack is injected  into the fibroblast and this leads to no  membrane ruffling activity  point to be noted at this point all the  focal adhesions are very intact  now when rho was micro injected in the  fibroblast  focal adhesions and stress fibers  were  forming that means rho is somehow  involved in the later part of this  cellular movement  similar effects has been reported when  lpa is injected into the cells that  means they can work in a common pathway  all these set of experiments allows the  scientists to construct a pathway  of how these cell movement can be  regulated so there are set of  extracellular factors such as growth  factors tropic factors which leads to  cellular movement binds to cell membrane  receptors on the cell that is undergoing  movement  and  these  factors ultimately lead to the  production or activation of this  monomeric g protein g protein such as  cdc42 rack or rho each of these g  protein has their own activity for  example cdc42 is involved in phylopodia  formation whereas rack  interacts with the actin network and  thereby regulating lamellipodia  formation and membrane ruffle formation  whereas rho interacts with myosin and  helps in the formation of stress fibers  and the focal adhesion  also  crucial experiments has shown that  these gtpases also work in a  hierarchical fashion such as cdc42  regulates rack and rack can regulate row  and thereby  overall this network can regulate how  the cell locomotion can take place  and thereby we are understand what are  the overall  cellular and molecular players involved  the cell locomotion process  so in summary we looked at the stages of  um cell locomotion and we took fish  keratinocyte as one example of a fast  moving cell  and we also looked at the signaling  factors and regulators that  are involved in cell locomotion if you  like this video give it a quick thumbs  up don't forget to like share and  subscribe thank you
 

